    <h2>What makes Level 43 tricky<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The opening card is the only negative instruction in this run: do not drag the magic wand to the man. Every other card says where something goes, this one says where it must not go, so the first decision is about the man rather than about the wand. After that the wand visits eleven targets in a fixed order — dirty floor, coffee, teddy bear, laptop, check-in calendar, cat, wardrobe, drawer under the wardrobe, fried chicken and soda, notebook under the table, begging girl. One object, eleven destinations, and the list is not grouped by room.</p>
    <p>Two destinations are nested. The drawer is under the wardrobe and comes one card later, so the wardrobe has to be dealt with before the drawer is in reach, and the notebook waits under the table until after the food. Two others are not things you would think to point a wand at: a check-in calendar on the wall and a tray of fried chicken and soda. That is the shape of the level, a sweep of one room in which the wand is the only tool that changes anything.</p>
    <p>The last two cards break the pattern twice. Step 12 points the wand at the begging girl, the only person on the eleven-target list, so the hand is still holding a wand when the level changes tools. Step 13 drags a bouquet to the man, the same man the first card told you to keep the wand away from. Twelve moves of one object and one destination each, then a different object to a person, which is why the ending slips: nothing about a bouquet looks like the next step in a wand sequence.</p>

    <h2>Wand targets in order<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Twelve of the thirteen cards use one object, and the order is the puzzle:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The man. He is the target the first card rules out and the one who receives the bouquet at the end.</li>
      <li>The dirty floor, then the coffee, then the teddy bear, in that order (steps 2 to 4).</li>
      <li>The laptop at step 5 and the check-in calendar at step 6.</li>
      <li>The cat, then the wardrobe, then the drawer under the wardrobe (steps 7 to 9).</li>
      <li>The fried chicken and soda, which is a target rather than a prop (step 10).</li>
      <li>The notebook under the table, then the begging girl, the only person the wand touches (steps 11 and 12).</li>
      <li>The bouquet at step 13: a different object, and the only move that ends on the man.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Keep a finger on the wand until step 12, then put it down, because the level finishes with flowers.</p>

    <h2>FAQ<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
   
    <details><summary>What is the answer to Brain Puzzle 3 Level 43?</summary><p>In order: 1) Do not drag the magic wand to the man; 2) Drag the magic wand to the dirty floor; 3) Drag the magic wand to the coffee; 4) Drag the magic wand to the teddy bear; 5) Drag the magic wand to the laptop; 6) Drag the magic wand to the check-in calendar; 7) Drag the magic wand to the cat; 8) Drag the magic wand to the wardrobe; 9) Drag the magic wand to the drawer under the wardrobe; 10) Drag the magic wand to the fried chicken and soda; 11) Drag the magic wand to the notebook under the table; 12) Drag the magic wand to the begging girl; 13) Drag the bouquet to the man</p></details>
    <details><summary>Why does nothing happen when I tap in Level 43?</summary><p>Every card in Level 43 is a drag, including the first one, which is a drag you are told not to make, and the object that changes at the end is the bouquet rather than the wand.</p></details>
